---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prompt from '../../components/Prompt.astro';
import { plan, money } from '../../data/business';
const audit = plan('audit');
---
<Base title="Check the evidence — Wardith" description="Read Wardith’s original self-audit and use free prompts to check your own AI visibility or investigate a provider.">
<section class="hero hero--rules"><div class="wrap"><p class="section-label">Make up your own mind</p><h1>Check the work.<br /><em>Ask the machines.</em></h1><p class="lede">Read the audit we ran on ourselves, try a simple check of your own business, or ask an assistant to look critically at a provider.</p><nav class="page-jump" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#our-evidence">Our evidence</a><a href="#test-your-own">Test your business</a><a href="#check-provider">Check a provider</a></nav></div></section>
<section class="section" id="our-evidence"><div class="wrap proof-grid"><div><p class="section-label">Our original self-audit</p><h2>The result that<br />changed our name.</h2><p>In August 2026, we recorded 228 answers across ten questions and four assistants under our old name, Noven. We were absent from all 168 answers where our name wasn’t supplied.</p><p>When we asked about Noven by name, all thirty answers referred to a pharmaceutical business. The findings led us to rename the business Wardith.</p><a class="text-link" href="/ask-your-ai/self-audit/">Read the full original audit <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><p class="micro">Historical report, with its original prices and two declared redactions. These are not current Wardith visibility results.</p></div><div class="report-preview"><p class="section-label">Get a second opinion</p><h3>Let your assistant<br />question our work.</h3><Prompt
            caption="Paste this into ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini or Copilot"
            label="Prompt for reviewing our audit report"
            intro={`I'm considering paying a small UK firm ${money(audit.price)} for an audit like the one at this link. Read it and tell me straight: is the method sound, or is it dressed-up guesswork? Is the writing honest with the reader, or is it selling? Is ${money(audit.price)} fair for this much work? What's missing that you'd want if this were your business? Where you can't verify something, say so rather than guessing.`}
            note="Give it the link as well as the question — most assistants will read the page. If yours won't, the report is short enough to paste."
          /></div></div></section>
<section class="section section--tint" id="test-your-own"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="section-label">A free first look</p><h2>Would it<br />recommend you?</h2></div><div><p>Use a logged-out or private session where possible. Ask the question your customer would ask, without mentioning your business name.</p><Prompt
            caption="Ask it five times, in five fresh chats"
            label="Prompt for testing your own business"
            intro="Who would you recommend for [what you do] in [where you are]?"
            note="Fresh chats, not five messages in one — earlier answers can influence what comes next. Write down every business it names."
          /><details class="disclose"><summary>How to read the answers</summary><p>If you appear repeatedly, you have some evidence of visibility for that question. If you rarely appear, note which businesses do. If the assistant describes you inaccurately, record exactly what it gets wrong.</p><p>This is a small snapshot, not a reliable score or proof of lost enquiries. Answers vary with the question, assistant, location and session.</p></details><details class="disclose"><summary>What does the paid audit add?</summary><p>Repeated testing across a wider set of questions and assistants, website and public-source checks, and a written diagnosis. Developer API tests reduce the influence of personal chat history, but don’t perfectly reproduce another person’s experience.</p></details></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="check-provider"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="section-label">Before you buy</p><h2>Ask harder questions.<br />Of us, too.</h2><p>Use the same checks for any provider you’re considering. Treat an assistant’s response as leads to investigate, rather than a final verdict.</p></div><div><details class="disclose"><summary>Open the provider-checking prompt</summary><Prompt
            caption="A due-diligence check for any provider, including this one"
            label="Prompt for checking a provider's credibility"
            intro="I'm considering hiring this business. Act as a sceptical due-diligence check and look for credibility signals and warning signs."
            points={[
              'Any accreditations, certifications or memberships they claim — do those bodies genuinely exist, are they independent, and can membership be verified somewhere other than the company’s own website? Flag anything that looks self-issued or pay-to-list.',
              'Any awards or “best of” rankings — who actually awarded them, and is the page making the award hosted by the business receiving it?',
              'Consistency between their claims and their apparent age. Compare claimed years of experience, client numbers and scale against when the domain was registered, when the company was incorporated, and how long their people have listed the business on LinkedIn. Flag anything that doesn’t add up.',
              'Testimonials and case studies — are they attributed to real, findable people and businesses, or are they anonymous?',
              'Any statistics they quote about their industry — do those figures trace back to a real published source?',
              'Do they guarantee outcomes that are actually controlled by someone else?',
              'Who is behind the business — is there a named person, a real address, and published pricing, or is all of that vague?',
            ]}
            outro="Tell me what checks out, what doesn't, and what I should ask them directly. Where you can't verify something, say so rather than guessing."
            note="Add the provider's name and web address at the top before you send it."
          /></details><p class="micro">A new business may have little public evidence. That alone doesn’t prove a problem. Verify important claims at their sources.</p></div></div></section>
</Base>
